A||N||4||Taijiguy||911  Flywheel woes! Help!||||1||
Z||000000||Taijiguy||04-11-2001||10:21 PM||||Went out to start my car.....WRRRRRRRR!!!<P>Repplaced starter: WRRRRRRRRRRR<P>Pulled new starter....ring gear is loose on the flywheel!  ARGGGGH!<P>Question: How much risk of more severe damage is there if I climb under there, and tack the ring ear back on in several spots with my light duty arc welder. I know it won;t be perfectly balanced, but what's the consensus on if it will possibly cause more damage until I can replace the flywheel....maybe a couple thousand miles at most. Thoughts?<P>~Taiji<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 04-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||04-11-2001||11:12 PM||||I do things to my own Fiero that I cannoot bring myself to recommend to others.<P>I would most likely do it as a temporary fix and then regret it mightily when it cracked apart later on down the road, working so well I never finished doing it correctly.<P>Hope the sparks don't turn it into another fiery Fiero statistic.||||reg||12
Z||000002||theogre||04-11-2001||11:34 PM||||I would not even begin to mess with this. The damage potential is far greater than the cost of replacing the wrecked flywheel.<P>The first thing you need to do is inspect the ring to make sure it's not broken. If the ring is broken in addition to being loose you need to replace it now.<P>While I would not recomend this,,,, If you absolutly have to patch it...<P>1. mark out at least 6 equally spaced spots around the gear. measure them twice...<P>2. Keep your welds very close the the same size. measure them to make sure.<P>How long this would hold only god knows. How well it will balance depends on how good a welder you are.<P>How much damage you will do it the balance is off depends how far off.... more than a couple grams and the damage to the rear main bearing could be extensive.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||04-12-2001||12:51 AM||||Well, truth is, I ain't much of a welder. I mean, I CAN weld OK, but this little thing I got ain't quite heavy enough for this kind of work. (Little 120 volt SIP welder) I used the pressure plate mounting bolts as reference marks for my welds. I tried to keep them primarily spot welds, not really doing a bead. I figure I'll go in afterwards and grind down the excess with my dremel, and pray the damn thing don't grenade on me. At least the ring isn't cracked, not from what I can tell, but I'll inspect it more closely tomorrow.<BR>Thanks for the responses.<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||04-12-2001||05:09 AM||||As with all things automotive the options come down to the same two things...<P>Replace the flywheel now and just eat the cost <P>OR<P>Try to Mickey Mouse the flywheel now and just hope that it won't cost triple the amount if it doesn't work...<P>We've all had to make this decision on our automotive problems. <BR>We've all paid the penalty for Option 2. We've all snickered at dodging the bullet of Option 2.<P>I have felt the lovely vibrations of an unbalance flywheel (85-87 flywheel on an 88 crank) and although it would have been great for shaking (not stirring) drinks it was pretty damn scary!<P>Good luck to you! Hope to hear you snickering soon!<BR>||||reg||6
